<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<subsection xml:id="subsec-num-project-results">
  <title>Results and Interpretation</title>

  <p>
    Explain what the statistical results mean for the three research
    questions. Report the relevant numerical findings and connect them
    to the populations, variables, and units in your study.
  </p>

  <paragraphs xml:id="num-project-result-one-mean">
    <title>One-Mean Analysis</title>
    <p>
      If you chose a confidence interval, report the sample mean,
      confidence level, and endpoints with units. Interpret the interval
      as an estimate of the population mean.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you chose a hypothesis test, report the sample mean, benchmark,
      <m>t</m> statistic, degrees of freedom, and <m>p</m>-value or critical value. CState whether you
      reject or do not reject <m>H_0</m>, and explain what the decision
      means for the original question. Match the conclusion to the
      direction of the alternative hypothesis.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="num-project-result-two-means">
    <title>Paired or Independent Two-Mean Test</title>
    <p>
      For a paired test, report and interpret the mean paired difference
      using the stated subtraction order. Explain whether there is
      sufficient evidence of the change or difference specified in the
      alternative hypothesis. A negative difference must be interpreted
      using the order in which the measurements were subtracted.
    </p>
    <p>
      For an independent test, report the two sample means and their
      observed difference. Explain whether there is sufficient evidence
      of a difference in population means, or of the directional
      difference specified by your alternative. Identify the groups by
      name and keep their subtraction order consistent.
    </p>
    <p>
      In either case, report <m>t</m>, degrees of freedom, the
      <m>p</m>-value or critical vaule, and the statistical decision. Explain the size
      of the observed difference in the measurement units as well as
      whether it is statistically significant.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="num-project-result-anova">
    <title>One-Way ANOVA and Follow-Up Comparisons</title>
    <p>
      Report the group sample means, <m>F</m> statistic, both degrees
      of freedom, and the ANOVA <m>p</m>-value. State the decision and
      answer the question about the population means.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the ANOVA is significant, interpret the Tukey comparisons.
      Identify the pairs with adjusted <m>p</m>-values below the
      significance level and describe the direction and observed size
      of those differences. If no individual pair is significant after
      adjustment, report that finding accurately. For other pairs,
      describe the evidence as insufficient rather than concluding
      that their population means are identical.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the ANOVA is not significant, explain that there is insufficient
      evidence of a difference among the population means and state
      that follow-up comparisons are not pursued for this project.
      This outcome can receive full credit when the analysis and
      explanation are correct.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>
</subsection>
